te who served several sultans and was a disciple of the Sufi saint Nizamuddin Auliya.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/amira-dubbaj"><span class="enc-name">Amira Dubbaj</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Amira Dubbaj, also called Muzaffar Sultan, was the Ishaqid ruler of western Gilan, on the Caspian coast of Iran, in the early 16th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/amirids"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/amirids.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Amirids</span><span class="enc-type">State</span><span class="enc-snip">The Amirids were the family of al-Mansur (Almanzor), the chief minister who ruled the Caliphate of Córdoba in the name of Hisham II from the 970s.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/amiriya-madrasa"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/amiriya-madrasa.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Amiriya Madrasa</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Amiriya Madrasa is a religious school and mosque in Rada'a, Yemen, built by the Tahirid sultan Amir ibn Abd al-Wahhab and completed in 1504.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/amiternum"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/amiternum.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Amiternum</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Amiternum was an ancient Sabine town in the upper Aterno valley of central Italy, near modern L'Aquila.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/al-amjad-bahramshah"><span class="enc-name">Al-Amjad Bahramshah</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Al-Amjad Bahramshah was the Ayyubid emir of Baalbek from 1182 to 1230, son of Saladin's nephew Farrukh Shah.</span></a></