nd in 1961 on the citadel hill of Amman, Jordan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ammianus-marcellinus"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ammianus-marcellinus.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ammianus Marcellinus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ammianus Marcellinus was a Greek-born Roman soldier and historian.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ammishtamru-i"><span class="enc-name">Ammishtamru I</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ammishtamru I was king of Ugarit in the fourteenth century BCE, during the Amarna period.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ammishtamru-ii"><span class="enc-name">Ammishtamru II</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ammishtamru II was king of Ugarit in the thirteenth century BCE, ruling as a vassal of the Hittite Empire at the height of the city's prosperity.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ammonius-saccas"><span class="enc-name">Ammonius Saccas</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ammonius Saccas was a Greek philosopher who taught in Alexandria in the first half of the third century CE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ammunira"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ammunira.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ammunira</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ammunira was the ruler of Beirut in the mid-14th century BCE, a vassal of the Egyptian pharaoh.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/amnisos"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/amnisos.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Amnisos</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Amnisos is 